  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000047",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Atom economy",
    "reading_order" : 47,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "heading",
    "heading_level" : 4,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000048",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The atom economy of a reaction shows how many of the atoms used in the reaction become the desired product",
    "reading_order" : 48,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000049",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The rest of the atoms or mass is wasted",
    "reading_order" : 49,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000050",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "It is found directly from the balanced equation by calculating the *M*<sub>r</sub> of the desired product",
    "reading_order" : 50,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000051",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "$Atomeconomy=\\frac{molecularmassofdesiredproduct}{sumofmolecularmassesofALLreactants}\\times100$ In addition reactions, the atom economy will always be 100% because all of the atoms are used to make the desired product Whenever there is only one product, the atom economy will always be 100% For example, in the reaction between ethene and bromine:",
    "reading_order" : 51,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000052",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub> + Br<sub>2</sub> → CH<sub>2</sub>BrCH2Br",
    "reading_order" : 52,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000053",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The atom economy could also be calculated using mass, instead or *M*<sub>r</sub>",
    "reading_order" : 53,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000054",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "In this case, you would divide the mass of the desired product formed by the total mass of all reactants, and then multiply by 100",
    "reading_order" : 54,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
